What is a Linked List?
A linked list is a linear data structure where elements (called nodes) are stored in a sequence, but not necessarily in contiguous memory locations.

Each node has two parts:
• Data – the actual value stored.
• Pointer (or reference) – a link to the next node in the sequence.
• Unlike arrays, linked lists don’t need continuous memory blocks, so they allow
dynamic memory allocation, efficient insertion and deletion, but slower random access.

A linked list is like a chain of connected items where you can easily insert or remove elements without shifting the rest.

Example 1: Train Coaches
• Imagine a train: each coach (node) has passengers (data) and a connector (pointer) that links it to the next coach.
• If you want to add a new coach, you just connect it to the existing chain without rearranging all coaches.

Example 2: Playlist of Songs
• Each song (node) contains the track (data) and a link to the next song (pointer).
• You can skip, add, or remove songs without shifting the entire playlist, just by changing links.